{
    "word": "bicameralism",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "b1739c7d-1ad9-3d79-8f7b-045c1d622af1",
                "word": "bicameralism",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "131053e6-bce9-4ec0-9846-0d051108d171",
                        "entry_id": "b1739c7d-1ad9-3d79-8f7b-045c1d622af1",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "bicameralisms"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "254013c5-f0ea-3a1f-994f-c26e3354f195",
                        "entry_id": "b1739c7d-1ad9-3d79-8f7b-045c1d622af1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The practice of dividing legislative bodies into two chambers with complementary powers and limitations designed to provide checks and balances against one another."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable",
                            "usually"
                        ],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "297ba7ee-f916-3df7-be02-405b1f77d615",
                        "entry_id": "b1739c7d-1ad9-3d79-8f7b-045c1d622af1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Ambiguous misnomer for Julian Jaynes's theory of bicamerality, probably never used by Jaynes, rarely used in academic literature based on his work, but often found informally (compare bicameral mind and bicameral mentality)"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable",
                            "usually"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8e395de2-55f2-4e8c-b1ec-e1d5b138c93a",
                                "sense_id": "297ba7ee-f916-3df7-be02-405b1f77d615",
                                "text": "1978, Edward Proffitt, \"Romanticism, Bicamerality, and the Evolution of the Brain\", The Wordsworth Circle, Vol. 9, No.1, reprinted with permission in Kuijsten, 2016, page 134.\nColeridge...could not revert to bicameralism either."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "82fa6053-510b-43b3-b09f-1c722b38860f",
                                "sense_id": "297ba7ee-f916-3df7-be02-405b1f77d615",
                                "text": "As a result of this bicameralism, they were not conscious as we are today, and had no unified sense of self."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fd41a65e-da9b-4a5f-9a05-d6590aa0eeb2",
                                "sense_id": "297ba7ee-f916-3df7-be02-405b1f77d615",
                                "text": "\"...the evidence for bicameralism in early China.\" p. 2\n\"...to elucidate the evidence for bicameralism and the transition to consciousness...\" p. 3\n\"...presents detailed evidence for bicameralism and the development of consciousness...\" p. 14"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "49837c90-2a0a-4be7-bb60-2550fba33a55",
                        "source_entry_id": "b1739c7d-1ad9-3d79-8f7b-045c1d622af1",
                        "source_sense_description": "principle of dividing legislative body into two groups",
                        "target_lang_word": "bicaméralisme",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "bicaméralisme",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "99375fb7-a1ff-3b38-a0e5-d02d73f76a99",
                                        "entry_id": "459e7366-7e9f-3de7-8c8d-afd345ee2401",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Système parlementaire qui réunit dans deux chambres des représentants du peuple."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "14b5f1bd-2fdb-4549-8e7f-340854717fdb",
                                                "sense_id": "99375fb7-a1ff-3b38-a0e5-d02d73f76a99",
                                                "text": "Le bicaméralisme est le système parlementaire le plus fréquent dans les démocraties, avec la plupart du temps une chambre haute (souvent appelée Sénat) et une chambre basse (au nom très variable : Assemblée nationale, Chambre des représentants ou des communes, Bundestag, etc.)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7c8487b9-60ea-3e0f-b9c7-973e55b19133",
                                        "entry_id": "459e7366-7e9f-3de7-8c8d-afd345ee2401",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Système qui joint des représentants du peuple à une instance dirigeante."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "figuratively"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "80d714f7-5679-49d4-a526-db9fbbb3eb29",
                                                "sense_id": "7c8487b9-60ea-3e0f-b9c7-973e55b19133",
                                                "text": "Isabelle Ferreras a quant à elle défendu l’idée d’un véritable bicaméralisme dans les entreprises, avec obligation pour le conseil des actionnaires et le conseil des salariés de se mettre d’accord et d’adopter les mêmes textes et décisions stratégiques."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
